@meta
id: 08-15
title: Drill — duplicate and bump
chapter: 08
type: drill
skills: yank-y, put-p, replace-r
par: 32
minutes: 5
reps: 10
vscode: copy line, paste, edit the digit, three times
open: routes.ts

@brief
Every v1 route needs a v2 twin directly underneath it.

The rep is five keys: copy the line, put it below, land on the version
digit, replace it.

@keys
| `yy` | yank the line |
| `p` | put it below |
| `f1` | forward to the `1` |
| `r2` | replace it with a `2` |
| `j` | down a line |

@hints
`yyp` gives you the duplicate. `f1r2` fixes it. `j` gets you to the next
original.
--
After `p` the cursor is already on the new line, so `f1` finds the
version digit of the copy and never the one above it.

@solution
    j  then three times: yyp f1r2 j  :wq<Enter>

Five keys per line, and the path itself is never retyped — which matters
more than the keystrokes, because a mistyped route fails at runtime and
not at compile time.

`yyp` is the duplicate-this-line gesture. Along with `ddp` from the
earlier drill it covers most of what people use a mouse for in a config
file: copy this down, move this up. Neither one needs a selection and
neither one can pick up the wrong number of lines.

@start routes.ts
export const routes = [
  '/v1/orders',
  '/v1/customers',
  '/v1/refunds',
];

@expected routes.ts
export const routes = [
  '/v1/orders',
  '/v2/orders',
  '/v1/customers',
  '/v2/customers',
  '/v1/refunds',
  '/v2/refunds',
];
